## TIẾNG ANH · 16:30–17:00 — Thứ Năm: Câu hỏi phỏng vấn

### 16:30–16:40 · Shadowing

### 16:40–16:55 · Trả lời có ghi âm

**Câu hỏi tuần này:** *"Explain the React Native New Architecture. Why does it matter?"*

> *"The old architecture had a bridge between JavaScript and native. Everything crossing it had to be serialised to JSON, every call was asynchronous, and all native modules were instantiated at startup.*
> *The new architecture replaces that with JSI, which lets JavaScript hold a direct reference to C++ objects. No serialisation, and synchronous calls are possible when you need them. On top of JSI you get TurboModules, which load lazily, and Fabric, the new renderer that supports concurrent React.*
> *The part I find most valuable in practice is Codegen. You write a TypeScript spec, and it generates the native interfaces for both platforms. So a signature mismatch is a compile error rather than a runtime crash. In the old system you wrote all three sides by hand and kept them in sync yourself.*
> *I built a TurboModule from scratch this week — spec, Codegen, Kotlin on Android, Swift with an Objective-C++ bridge on iOS. Then I rebuilt the same thing with the Expo Modules API, which took about a third of the code. I verified lazy loading myself by logging in the constructor: nothing fires until the screen that uses it opens.*
> *It's been the default since React Native 0.76, and around eighty-five percent of popular libraries support it now — so the practical question for a team isn't whether to adopt it, it's auditing which of your dependencies are still bridge-only."*

**Câu trả lời này mạnh vì có ba tầng:** khái niệm → điều bạn tự làm → tình hình thực tế của thị trường. Rất ít ứng viên Middle nói được tầng thứ hai.

